feat(cli): innsegl retire — let an operator end a run (#257)

Nothing in this system can observe that an agent died. The reaper sees
silence, which is a withdrawal of standing authorisation and not an
ending; a harness's stop hook speaks for the process it owned. An
operator who knows the machine is gone had no way to put that into the
record without an MCP client.

`innsegl retire <run_id>` is a client of `retire_agent` on the
identity-lifecycle listener and nothing more: no tool, event type,
field, enum or error class changes. Four exit statuses because an
operator acts differently on each — ended (0), already ended (18), no
such run (19), unreachable (20).

"Already ended" is derived rather than refused: IP §4 makes the tool
idempotent, so it answers a retired run with success and the ORIGINAL
instant and never refuses one. The command notes the instant it asks
and compares; the limit of that is written down where it is done.

Driven by MCP-086, which covers the four outcomes over a real MCP
transport against a stand-in for the admin listener.

